1. `conversion of any char to ascii value` : `int x = 'a';` then this won't throw error this will smartly
    typecase our char a into its corresponding ascii value and that is 97 in the variable x , and even when
    subtraction char from char of char from number `'a' - 'a' or 5- 'a'` both will return us a integer.
    
    
2. `What hashing actually is and why we use hashing` : Whenever we have asked any element's appearance a lots and lots
    of time from a array or list and then we can use builtin count functions and then return that value that this won't
    efficient for big and big array or list for that then another approach we can use that for that given array or lists 
    counting elements we will create a array(size equal to that given list and initialize all indices to 0) and that array
    indicies will act as that element and that indicies value will act as the number of times that element appear and
    then we can print . And then as per condition we can increment or decrement the indices value as per the condition
    and we can manipulate as per requirement .
    
    
3. `What we will do then` : hashing is basically prestoring and fetching . We will create a another array that will be hash
    array of frequency array then we will initialize all indices of array with 0 and then we will alot all indices from 1 to
    size -1 of this hash array with that corresponding arrays frequency means whenever my original question given array will
    have an element suppose 1 then i will increase index 1 in my hash array and in this way i calculate all frequencies and
    then at last i can print this array from 1 to n-1. And make sure to create hash array of required size + 1 as will start
    fetcthing from index 1. 
    
    
4. `Some limits of hashing array` : for integral hashing array we can declare size upto 10^6   inside main function and in global
    you can declare upto 10^7 + 1 means around 10^7 and for booleans inside main 10^7 and in global 10^8 . make sure whenever
    you are initializing any array globally that will be initialized to 0(for integers) and not with garbage values.
    
    
5. `integral hashing` : create a hashing array the maximum size you want to hash + 1 and then hash as per the inputs you get
    from the original array and then after get hashed array do the asked computation with that hash array.
    
    
6. `character hashing` : if we wanted to know how many times a particular asked character in a array is present then we
    can create a hash or frequency array of characters. And suppose all characters in the string are lowercase letter
    only and we know that there will be 26 possible letters and so i can create a vector array of size 26(0 to 25) and
    then in my mind i will asume a will corresponds to 0 , b to 1 , ... z to 25 this won't be written anywhere we considered
    our hash arrays indices in this manner . Then for this purpose we have to use ascii (as when we write ` int x = 'a';`
    this will store the ascii value of a and that is 97 means this will typecase instead of throwing error) . And by char-'a'
    (this will do subraction between ascii key and return a integer value) and that value can be used to hash that char in
    the hash array .          `e[d[i]-'a']++;` e is our hash array and d is the array whose characters we wanted to hash
    then we can hash in this way and `char a;cin >> a; e[a-'a'] ` this way we can access frequency of char a from our hashing
    array(e). Otherwise you can create hash array to size 256(0 to 255) and initialize with 0 and now you can cover all
    uppercase and lower case characters directly and no need for subtraction just direct conversion through ascii values would
    be enough. And we are not subtracting then we can put values in hash array as `e[d[i]]++;` this will auto convert because
    program will take as we are operating on a index of e that is d[i] and d[i] is a character then to make sense compiler
    will auto convert our char to its corresponding ascii value  and we can access using `e[a];`.
    
    
7. `how map work for hashing` : using maps over direct array hashing sometimes more smarter decision . as `map<int,int> d;` 
    then this first int will act as the element(key) and other int will act as the frequency(corresponding value to the key).
    As when we will look in `d[1]++;` if 1 doesn't exist then compiler will automatically assign 0 as value and we operated
    on that value and incremented then we will get 1 and now whenever again 1 repeats its value will get 2,3,.... basically
    number of times the 1 repeating.  So from array to hashing our frequency directly using maps then we can use `m[a[i]]++;`
    then this will access our ith element of array a then thsi will has its frequency in our m map `map<int,int> m;` this one . 
    
    
8. `Chosing between hashing array or map` : when there are only limited element's whose frequency we wanted to find then use map
    because suppose we have integers 1, 2, 13 then instead of creating a array of 14 size i will use map that will take only 3
    elements as input and for this scenario our maps will be more optimised solution.
    
    
9. `how to use map` : when doing hashing for integer then your key is integer and for character your key is character like 
    `map<int,int> m; map<char,int> n;` here m is the hashing map for integers and n for characters. And in map `n[d[i]]++` 
    then our map won't change ith character from d vector into integer because we already declared our key of the map as 
    character so here we stopped auto conversion and instead provided the required type already. 
    
    
10. `Which map to use for fetching` : if order doesn't matter then unordered map because average time complexity of unordered map for
      inserting and accessing any element average is O(1) and worst is O(N) that is total number of elements in the unordered map
      wherease for map both average and worst complexity if O(log(n)) and is the n of elements in the map.
